# Linux course notes

Use this rule for Linux only. Trigger: **"apply the notes_doc_linux rule"**, or any request to document a Linux video or lab under `Personal/Files/`. Do not copy the Lab / Terminal Commands / Code child accordions from `notes_doc.mdc`.

## Hub

`Personal/Files/linux.md` is a link list. A lab row links to that course's index, same shape as the AWS lab row in `Personal/Files/aws_ai.md`. Do not paste the course into `linux.md`.

```markdown
- [ ] [**-- LAB --** | Course Title](https://github.com/omeatai/Personal/blob/main/Files/linux/01.md)
```

## Layout

Mirror `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`, `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/`, and `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/images/`.

| Course | Index | Chapters | Figures | Video and sibling `.txt` transcript |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linux Command Line - Entire Tutorial Series | `Personal/Files/linux/01.md` | `Personal/Files/linux/command-line/md/NNN-title.md` | `Personal/Files/linux/command-line/images/NNN/1.jpg` | `Personal/course_videos/Linux Command Line - Entire Tutorial Series/` |
| Linux Security & Administration From Boot Process to Firewalls | `Personal/Files/linux/02.md` | `Personal/Files/linux/security/md/NNN-title.md` | `Personal/Files/linux/security/images/NNN/1.jpg` | `Personal/course_videos/Linux Security & Administration From Boot Process to Firewalls/` |

`NNN` is the chapter number padded to 3 digits. From a chapter file, figures are `../images/NNN/1.jpg`. The contents link is `../../01.md` or `../../02.md`.

## Course index

Match `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`:

- Back link to `Files/linux.md`
- `#` course title
- `## [COURSE](url)` using the YouTube or course URL already on the `linux.md` row or in the transcript
- `## Introduction`
- `## Table of contents` with sections; each chapter number links to its own file

## Chapter page

Accordion spacing is the spacing in `Personal/bin/format.md`: one blank line after `</summary>` and one blank line before `</details>`.

1. `[← Course contents](../../01.md)` (or `02.md`)
2. `# N. Title`, course link, transcript link, video time range
3. `## Introduction` (plain heading)
4. `## Detailed Explanation` (plain heading) and one `<details>` step per topic
5. Questions and Answers accordion — the only separate section, immediately before the summary
6. `## Summary` (plain heading)
7. `## References` (plain heading; first link is the course URL)

Inside each step, mix notes, commands, code, and figures. Do **not** add child accordions named Lab, Terminal Commands, or Code.

- `- [x]` notes for that step
- fenced `bash` for commands spoken in that step
- fenced code when the transcript shows config or file text
- at least one figure: `![**Figure 1.** caption](../images/NNN/1.jpg)`

Q&A answers use nested `<details><summary>Answer</summary>` and `- [x]` lines only.

## Source of truth

- Use the local video and its sibling transcript `.txt`. Do not invent flags, paths, or steps the transcript does not support.
- Write spoken commands as real commands (`apt dash get` → `apt-get`, `LS dash L A H` → `ls -lah`).
- If the video has embedded chapter markers, write one chapter doc per marker. Otherwise start a chapter where the transcript starts a new tutorial or lesson.
- Inside a chapter, start a new step when the command or topic changes.

## Figures

- Grab stills with ffmpeg from the local video. Do not download images.
- One frame per step: a command, man page, diagram, config file, or terminal result on screen, plus a frame at each chapter start.
- If a frame is black or blank, grab another frame a few seconds later.
- Do not save long runs of near-duplicate frames.
- Name files `1.jpg`, `2.jpg`, … in that chapter's image folder.

